[["Notes","passage-en-02810","20) The sequence of al-Tabari traditions that we have examined and continue to examine includes the legend of al-Hadr, the Reward of Sinimmar, and Jadhima."],["Notes","passage-en-02811","21) In Greek sources the name appears as Dioscurides."],["Notes","passage-en-02812","22) Taha Baqir, The Epic of Gilgamesh, Baghdad, p. 48."],["Notes","passage-en-02813","23) The editor’s note says: “What he means, and God knows best, is the violation by Quraysh of the prohibition against fighting there.” In my view, the editor’s interpretation only made an already difficult passage more obscure."],["Notes","passage-en-02814","24) Al-Ya‘qubi, History; Ibn Qutayba, Poetry and the Poets; al-Isfahani, al-Aghani; Ibn al-Athir, al-Kamil; and Yaqut al-Hamawi, Mu‘jam al-Buldan."],["Notes","passage-en-02815","25) That is, confined in prison."],["Notes","passage-en-02816","26) Surat al-Fatiha (2)."],["Notes","passage-en-02817","27) The name appears in this form in Sumerian epics and myths as a god of grain (Apachnan). There may therefore be some connection between this Sumerian deity and the title of the Hyksos king."],["Notes","passage-en-02818","28) We should remember that Imru’ al-Qays travelled to Byzantium and fell in love with the emperor’s daughter."],["Notes","passage-en-02819","29) In the legend of Imru’ al-Qays, we likewise find him fighting to rescue his wife Hind from her abductors."],["Notes","passage-en-02820","30) Diwan, p. 118."],["Indexes","en-indexes-indexes","Indexes"],["Indexes","en-indexes-index-of-names","Index of Names"],["Indexes","en-indexes-a-أ","A (أ)"],["Indexes","passage-en-02821","Abraha the Abyssinian — 41, 42, 133–137, 139"],["Indexes","passage-en-02822","Abu Righal — 133, 136, 137, 139"],["Indexes","passage-en-02822","Apollo — 58, 83"],["Indexes","passage-en-02823","Abu Talib — 29, 31, 97"],["Indexes","passage-en-02824","Abu Righal — 41, 133–140, 217"],["Indexes","passage-en-02825","Enoch — 18, 199, 200, 203, 208, 212"],["Indexes